CHANGE ANALYSIS (citations verified, indeterminate)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────
Cannot be established from this repository alone: the diff and surrounding context alone are not enough to say.
The serialization mechanism is directly shown, but the supplied context does not establish that any production route or caller returns response.Response, nor whether deployed responses populate Metadata. Consequently, real-user exposure cannot be determined from this diff alone.

HTTP handlers returning response.Response now preserve that value through the handler pipeline so the responder can serialize both its data and metadata, while custom headers continue to be applied. The resulting JSON includes a metadata property when populated, but currently emits metadata:null when Metadata is unset, contrary to the updated documentation's omission example.

Likely behavioral changes
  • A response.Response returned by a handler is now serialized as an HTTP envelope containing its Data and Metadata, rather than having only its Data forwarded for serialization. This makes caller-supplied metadata observable in the JSON response while retaining custom-header handling.
  • When Responder.Respond receives a response.Response with no Metadata value, its JSON body includes "metadata":null rather than omitting the property.

Investigate
  • Trace registered handlers and other call sites that return response.Response values to determine which HTTP APIs acquire the metadata-capable envelope behavior.
  • Inspect the complete response-writing path for status selection, error serialization, custom headers, and pointer-versus-value response handling.
  • Assess API consumers and schema contracts for compatibility with the new metadata property, especially the distinction between an absent property and a null value.

Uncertain
  • The supplied context does not identify which registered HTTP routes, if any, return response.Response and therefore expose the changed JSON shape.
  • It is not established which production handlers or non-route callers reach Responder.Respond with a response.Response value rather than another supported response type.
  • The diff does not establish whether real handler executions populate Metadata; populated metadata depends on the response values constructed at runtime.
  • The supplied files do not identify external clients, schemas, or middleware that depend on metadata being absent, null, or populated.
  • The wider context is needed to determine whether pointer values such as *response.Response are supported elsewhere or bypass both the custom-header and metadata-specific type assertions shown here.

